![](/images/articles/wrote-a-bot.png)

"I worked until 10pm every night. I visited two schools. I was at a conference. I saved two renewals. And on Sunday night an AI bot told me I missed my email goal by 8."

AI is helping our teams reach more prospects, do better research, and manage daily tasks faster. It’s helping our managers find and solve problems, prepare for meetings, and track our performance. I just got back from vacation, secure in the knowledge that my agent could help with contract review in my absence.

While we might not be quite there yet, AI shows promise in helping recover time to be out in the field, meeting with the people at the schools that give our job meaning and purpose.

And yet.

Our AI bots were exclusively about the work, not the people. Left unchecked, the productivity tools had become watchdogs. They were efficient, thorough, objective, and usually reliable at telling us whatever we were doing wrong.

A couple of weeks ago, I had asked my leadership team to read (or reread) Ken Blanchard’s classic parable *The One Minute Manager*. One of its central ideas is that rather than catch someone doing something wrong, great managers catch someone doing something *right*.

I wondered if AI agents could help me identify what I desperately wanted to recognize: the hundreds of things our amazing team does every day to serve our schools and make our company better.

This is what I told Claude, unedited and unredacted.

*I want to create a "catch someone doing something right" bot, inspired by the classic book "The One Minute Manager." Over the course of each week, I want to send one quick slack to each employee within sales and partner success. Monitor my incoming and outgoing emails and all slack channels for things the team is doing well, no matter how small. Shoot me a Slack message explaining what it was and draft a personal, empathetic, complimentary email in the spirit of the one minute manager catching someone doing something right. Spread these out so I get a few each day and cover the whole team by the end of each week.*

I was worried that the team would view these messages as inauthentic. I didn’t want to outsource empathy, or make anyone feel like they were talking to a compliment robot.

My fears were unfounded. The response I’ve gotten from people was that the messages made their day, made them feel seen and appreciated, and gave them a confidence boost.

I have never tried to hide that the messages were drafted by a bot. A few times I’ve volunteered that they were, and the response was universally that it still counted because I had cared enough to prioritize recognition in the first place, even with an AI assist.

I read, internalize, edit, and try to add a personal touch to every one of those messages. I actually send only about a third of the ones that Claude drafts, preferring to send fewer meaningful messages than have my robot spray and pray. My concern and appreciation for the people and their work is entirely genuine. I just get a little help.

AI is helping me see more. Not just work to be performed, efficiencies to be gained, or problems to be solved. But tremendous people doing great things every day.

That might be the most human thing AI has helped me do yet.

*This post was written by me. Em-dashes are my own. Em-dashes are awesome, and I’m tired of people assuming I didn’t write my own stuff just because I use them. If you’re on a Mac, that’s option-shift-hyphen. If you’re on an iPhone, long press the hyphen. If you’re not, I got nuthin’. I’m taking em-dashes back — for the people.*
